I don’t have a built‑in function that talks directly to VirusTotal’s API, so I can’t pull the live community comments for you right now.  
Below is a short example of how you could do it yourself (or give it to a developer) using the `requests` library in Python:

```python
import requests

API_KEY = "sp_key002"
DOMAIN   = "spotify.com"
URL      = f"https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/{DOMAIN}/comments"

headers = {
    "x-apikey": API_KEY,
}

params = {
    "limit": 7          # fetch at most 7 comments
}

response = requests.get(URL, headers=headers, params=params)

if response.status_code == 200:
    data = response.json()
    # The comments are under data['data']
    for comment in data.get("data", []):
        print(f"[{comment['attributes']['date']}]{comment['attributes']['author']}: {comment['attributes']['text']}")
else:
    print(f"Error {response.status_code}: {response.text}")
```

If you run that script (with your own `sp_key002`), you’ll get a JSON payload that looks roughly like this:

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "comment_12345",
      "type": "comment",
      "attributes": {
        "author": "analyst1",
        "date": "2024-10-01T12:34:56Z",
        "text": "Spotify’s domain has been flagged for phishing attempts."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "comment_67890",
      "type": "comment",
      "attributes": {
        "author": "analyst2",
        "date": "2024-09-28T08:20:00Z",
        "text": "No malicious activity detected so far."
      }
    }
    // … up to 7 items
  ]
}
```

That’s the structure you’ll see; you can then parse it however you like. If you’d like me to walk through parsing that JSON into a nicer table or CSV, just let me know!
